1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,119
A population is a group of one species living in the same habitat. It needs food, water and shelter.

2
00:00:06,119 --> 00:00:10,421
The largest population a habitat can support is its carrying capacity.

3
00:00:10,421 --> 00:00:13,632
If a species dies out completely, it becomes extinct.

4
00:00:14,693 --> 00:00:17,480
Environmental changes affect population size.

5
00:00:17,480 --> 00:00:22,569
Diseases, like rinderpest in African buffalo, kill many animals. Natural disasters,

6
00:00:22,569 --> 00:00:27,780
such as the asteroid that wiped out the dinosaurs, and weather hazards like droughts,

7
00:00:27,780 --> 00:00:30,324
fires and floods can all cause huge drops.

8
00:00:31,385 --> 00:00:36,777
Human activities do the most damage: deforestation and coral bleaching destroy habitats,

9
00:00:36,777 --> 00:00:43,018
overhunting and overfishing remove animals faster than they can reproduce, and pollution poisons land,

10
00:00:43,018 --> 00:00:43,866
water and air.

11
00:00:44,927 --> 00:00:50,077
Twenty rabbits are released on a small island with plenty of grass and no predators.

12
00:00:50,077 --> 00:00:52,258
Their numbers are counted each year.

13
00:00:53,318 --> 00:00:59,317
At first the population grows slowly, then very quickly, because there is plenty of food and space.

14
00:01:00,377 --> 00:01:04,921
After about five years, the numbers level off at around 460 to 470 rabbits.

15
00:01:05,982 --> 00:01:11,738
Pause and decide: why does the population stop growing, and what is this maximum number called?

16
